  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Back in the dining room, the French guests are singing La Marseillaise. Dmitry Nagiyev looks at the drunk chef Jacques and decides to taste the dish himself. We'll hear it in Russian first; don't worry about catching every word.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Исполняют Марсельезу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой приятный вечер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте извиним Жак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он, видимо, сильно накушался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Даже без нашего блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он очень устал сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поэтому он не может есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что ж.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда дегустатором буду я.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам попробую это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надеюсь, это вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Посмотрим, что приготовил повар.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's go through it line by line. Dmitry Nagiyev watches the singing guests.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Исполняют Марсельезу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They're singing La Marseillaise.' Notice how 'to perform' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'La Marseillaise'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой приятный вечер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a pleasant evening.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'what a' with 'evening'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте извиним Жак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's forgive Jacques.' The key word here means 'let's', which Dmitry combines with 'to excuse'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он, видимо, сильно накушался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He seems to have had too much.' The verb for 'got drunk' is in the masculine past tense, marked by that 'l' sound at the end.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Даже без нашего блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Even before our dish.' Dmitry combines 'even' with 'of dish' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он очень устал сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is very tired today.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'he' with 'today'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поэтому он не может есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Therefore he cannot eat.' Dmitry puts 'that's why' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'he'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что ж.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, then.' Notice how 'oh' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'on earth'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда дегустатором буду я.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then I will be the taster.' Notice how 'back then' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'I'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам попробую это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will try it myself.' Dmitry combines 'I' with 'this' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надеюсь, это вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope it's tasty.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'hope so' with 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Посмотрим, что приготовил повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's see what the chef cooked.' The verb for 'cooked' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending. Now let's hear the whole scene once more, start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
